[{"data":1,"prerenderedAt":608},["ShallowReactive",2],{"blog-list-2":3},{"posts":4,"totalPages":166},[5,190,275,544],{"id":6,"title":7,"author":8,"body":9,"date":169,"dateUpdated":170,"description":171,"excerpt":170,"extension":172,"headline":7,"meta":173,"navigation":174,"path":175,"seo":176,"socialImage":177,"stem":182,"tags":183,"__hash__":189},"blog/blog/publications.md","Publications","Michael Paulukonis",{"type":10,"value":11,"toc":164},"minimark",[12,20,25],[13,14,15],"p",{},[16,17],"img",{"alt":18,"src":19},"fragment of Backslash Lit No.4","/media/publications.thumb.png",[21,22,24],"h2",{"id":23},"places-i-can-or-have-been-found","Places I can or have been found",[26,27,28,40,48,55,63,70,77,97,106,119,127,138,147,156],"ul",{},[29,30,31,32,39],"li",{},"Perspective magazine published ",[33,34,38],"a",{"href":35,"rel":36},"https://abc.perspektive.at/obscurator-visually-difficult/",[37],"nofollow","OBSCURUS",", 2020/2021",[29,41,42,47],{},[33,43,46],{"href":44,"rel":45},"https://www.utsanga.it/paulukonis-works-a-short-anthology/",[37],"Utsanga",", March 2021",[29,49,50,54],{},[33,51,46],{"href":52,"rel":53},"https://www.utsanga.it/paulukonis-works/",[37],", Dec. 2020",[29,56,57,62],{},[33,58,61],{"href":59,"rel":60},"https://web.njit.edu/~newrev/3.0/paulukonis.html",[37],"5 Emotional Spam Poems"," in the Newark Review 3.0",[29,64,65],{},[33,66,69],{"href":67,"rel":68},"https://backslashlit.com/issues/4/michael-paulukonis",[37],"Backslash Lit #4",[29,71,72],{},[33,73,76],{"href":74,"rel":75},"https://backslashlit.com/issues/2/michael-j-paulukonis-hex-kilo-gram",[37],"Backslash Lit #2",[29,78,79,80],{},"Issac Karth's (late, lamented) Procedural Generation Blog has done an overview of a few things:\n",[26,81,82,89],{},[29,83,84],{},[33,85,88],{"href":86,"rel":87},"https://procedural-generation.isaackarth.com/2016/03/29/automated-fairy-tales.html",[37],"FairyTalesBot",[29,90,91,96],{},[33,92,95],{"href":93,"rel":94},"https://procedural-generation.isaackarth.com/2015/11/26/134033376013.html",[37],"LE Omelette D'ARTHUR"," 2013",[29,98,99,100,105],{},"included in ",[33,101,104],{"href":102,"rel":103},"https://www.laurenceking.com/product/instagram-poetry-for-every-day/",[37],"Instagram Poetry for Every Day",", Lawrence King publishing, 2020",[29,107,108,109,113,114],{},"The ",[110,111,112],"em",{},"Cream City Review's"," non-print journal ",[33,115,118],{"href":116,"rel":117},"https://uwm.edu/creamcityreview/cheshire-michael-paulukonis/",[37],"Chesire",[29,120,121,122],{},"Cover of ",[33,123,126],{"href":124,"rel":125},"https://petrichormag.com/petrichor-26/",[37],"Petrichor #26",[29,128,129,130,133,134],{},"part of Techspressionism group show ",[110,131,132],{},"TECHSPRESSIONISM - Digital & Beyond",", at Southampton Arts Center, 2022  ",[33,135,136],{"href":136,"rel":137},"https://techspressionism.com/exhibition/southampton/michael-paulukonis/",[37],[29,139,140,141,146],{},"included in the ",[33,142,145],{"href":143,"rel":144},"https://thewrong.org/Techspressionism",[37],"Techspressionism Pavillion"," at The Wrong Biennale 2023",[29,148,149,150,155],{},"visual poems in Jim Andrews' ",[33,151,154],{"href":152,"rel":153},"https://seaofpo.vispo.com?p=mp",[37],"Sea pf Po",", 2024",[29,157,99,158,163],{},[33,159,162],{"href":160,"rel":161},"https://techspressionism.com/brooklyn",[37],"\"Hello, Brooklyn!\" // Techspressionism 2024"," at the Kingsborough Art Museum, 2024",{"title":165,"searchDepth":166,"depth":166,"links":167},"",2,[168],{"id":23,"depth":166,"text":24},"2021-07-01T00:00:00.000Z",null,"Places that have graced me with my presence","md",{},true,"/blog/publications",{"title":7,"description":171},{"src":19,"mime":178,"alt":179,"width":180,"height":181},"png","an online web zine",586,413,"blog/publications",[184,185,186,187,188],"publications","writing","poetry","art","pinned","aCubK_VbE_rbHfsATi5_ALHws6dtYOmAeVeBdmgiw0s",{"id":191,"title":192,"author":8,"body":193,"date":256,"dateUpdated":170,"description":257,"excerpt":170,"extension":172,"headline":258,"meta":259,"navigation":174,"path":262,"seo":263,"socialImage":264,"stem":269,"tags":270,"__hash__":274},"blog/blog/polychrometext-introduction.md","Introducing PolychromeText",{"type":10,"value":194,"toc":253},[195,200,203,209,213,221,224,227,230,237,245],[196,197,199],"h1",{"id":198},"an-introduction-to-polychrometext","An introduction to PolychromeText",[13,201,202],{},"NOTE: as of 03.2025 this needs a massive update. As does the code.",[13,204,205],{},[16,206],{"alt":207,"src":208},"a working session of PolychromeText showing an older GUI","/media/polychrometext.thumb.jpg",[21,210,212],{"id":211},"what-are-you-talking-about","What are you talking about?",[13,214,215,220],{},[33,216,219],{"href":217,"rel":218},"https://michaelpaulukonis.github.io/polychrome.p5/",[37],"THIS"," is PolychromeText - a text-painter web-app.",[13,222,223],{},"Ever since I was a wee nipper, I've wanted to be able to create with text the way Jackson Pollock created with paint:\ndripping, flinging, pouring, etc.",[13,225,226],{},"Andy Warhol and Mark Rothko are also influences in various ways. Warhol's screen-prints led me to think how \"text\" is almost infinitely repeatable, and thus not a comodity, like a painting or a print could be. That led me down many roads with physical concrete poetry, typewriters and text paintings. Rothko is perhaps a more oblique influence, but he was me entree to color-field painting, and how several of my college classmates couldn't stand abstract paintings like that - their irritated descriptions filled me with a desire to create something similarly monolithic. I don't think I ever really did anything over-all until recently).",[13,228,229],{},"In early 2014 I took a \"MOOC\" on Processing, and one of my exercises became Polychrometext, so-named since it's second-week of life (it had a brief life as \"Text-Painter\" which is accurate if prosaic). I used the \"Javascript Mode\" to bring the sketch to the web. Eventually, I converted it to use Processing.js, and in 2018 moved on to p5js.",[13,231,232,233,236],{},"The app has continued to evolve, perhaps slowly, as I continue to simultaneously \"focus\" on both the output and the internals - I ",[110,234,235],{},"am"," a programmer during the day, and \"good\" code is important to me. Working on this project is a chance to express my viusal sense, but also exercise my code-skills. That said, I wouldn't necessarily hold it up as a stellar example of my work - it is highly unplanned and imperfect, filled with comments and side-tracks. Hopefully, it does show several things that I care about, and I would encourage anybody who wants to have a long-lived creative-coding project to realize that organizing the code makes it so much easier to grow your code.",[13,238,239,244],{},[33,240,243],{"href":241,"rel":242},"https://www.instagram.com/explore/tags/polychrometext/",[37],"Instragram posts",".",[13,246,247,248,244],{},"Most of them are also dumped into ",[33,249,252],{"href":250,"rel":251},"https://www.flickr.com/photos/othermichael/albums/72157641585339145",[37],"Flickr",{"title":165,"searchDepth":166,"depth":166,"links":254},[255],{"id":211,"depth":166,"text":212},"2019-09-14T16:00:00.000Z","A browser-based text-drawing program because I was jealous of Jackson Pollock and Mark Rothko","PolychromeText",{"authorUrl":260,"fmContentType":261},"https://michael.paulukonis.github.io/","default","/blog/polychrometext-introduction",{"title":192,"description":257},{"src":208,"mime":265,"alt":266,"width":267,"height":268},"webp","an older version of Polychrometext with some GUI elements",900,583,"blog/polychrometext-introduction",[187,271,272,188,273],"programming","text","p5js","_XYXvGuGQnZs_JsQ0ku7Z7bobdQiRQdhrVKbPmASO4c",{"id":276,"title":277,"author":8,"body":278,"date":528,"dateUpdated":529,"description":530,"excerpt":170,"extension":172,"headline":277,"meta":531,"navigation":174,"path":532,"seo":533,"socialImage":534,"stem":539,"tags":540,"__hash__":543},"blog/blog/automated-bots.md","Automated Art Automatons",{"type":10,"value":279,"toc":525},[280,294,296,306,309,317,320,323,336,340,344],[13,281,282,283,288,289],{},"NOTE: as of 03.2025 just about everything on this list is defunct, except for ",[33,284,287],{"href":285,"rel":286},"https://leanstooneside.tumblr.com/",[37],"ListMania"," and ",[33,290,293],{"href":291,"rel":292},"https://github.com/MichaelPaulukonis/PoeticalBot",[37],"PoeticalBot",[196,295,212],{"id":211},[297,298,299],"blockquote",{},[297,300,301],{},[297,302,303],{},[13,304,305],{},"Well I drove down to Big D.C.\nAnd I walked into Room 1003\nAnd there they were The Big Boys.\nAnd they were talking Big B Little O Little M Silent B\nThey were saying: '''Let's teach those robots how to play hardball.\nLet's teach those little fellas a little gratitude.'''\n-> Laurie Anderson, ''Sharkey's Night'', Home of the Brave, 1985",[13,307,308],{},"I've been writing bots for a while now. Some are still running.",[13,310,311,312,244],{},"I thought about this for over a year, until somebody goaded me into ",[33,313,316],{"href":314,"rel":315},"http://bbs.boingboing.net/t/comprehensively-addressing-the-stupid-intellectually-dishonest-critique-of-anita-sarkeesian/40229/139",[37],"actualizing a joke",[13,318,319],{},"I started out with Google-spreadhsheet-script bots, and \"graduated\" to NodeJs bots running on Heroku.",[13,321,322],{},"I have some ideas for future bots, but who knows. These things are fun, programming exercises, linguistic exercises, artistic expressions, artistic experiments, autistic experiments, and whoknowswhat.",[13,324,325,326,331,332],{},"Bots that use Tracery/",[33,327,330],{"href":328,"rel":329},"http://cheapbotsdonequick.com/",[37],"Cheap Bots Done Quick"," may have some source and notes @ ",[33,333,334],{"href":334,"rel":335},"https://github.com/MichaelPaulukonis/tracery-grammars",[37],[196,337,339],{"id":338},"bots","Bots",[21,341,343],{"id":342},"quick-list","quick list",[26,345